y. Compare this to the cost of one 19-day outage in lost revenue."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What should a contract with a model provider include?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Contracts should specify: (1) SLA for availability and mean-time-to-recovery if service is suspended, (2) minimum notice period for service changes or suspension (48–72 hours is reasonable; hours/minutes is not), (3) liability cap if suspension is not due to your breach (ideally capped at monthly spend or an agreed amount), (4) right to audit export-control compliance and regulatory risk, (5) fallback terms: does the provider have contractual obligation to help route to a competitor if they are blocked by regulation? Most vendor contracts today lack these entirely. Add them now, before concentration risk becomes a crisis."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you model the cost of a provider outage?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Start with: (1) monthly AI-dependent revenue from this provider's workloads (e.g., $250k/month), (2) outage duration (the Fable 5 outage was 19 days), (3) fraction of revenue that would have been at-risk (assume 80–100% of the workload routes through the suspended model), (4) cost of fallback (if you have one: cost delta × volume), (5) cost of no fallback (lost revenue, customer SLA penalties, engineering fire-fighting). A company with $250k/month in Fable 5–dependent revenue loses ~$160k–$200k in a 19-day outage (after accounting for partial fallback and some traffic shifting). Multiply by your company's numbers, then decide if provider diversity is worth $20k–$40k/year in maintenance costs."}}]}
